    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_5bacf36a4bde10bc6aa57f120e9a8fcf1abb8e668b93e84a0aa3dabc772c5923",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "The corrected mean yields a positive linear aggregate and hence infinitely many positive sampled values at Gram ordinates, but the argument-principle route needs positivity continuously along an entire top boundary segment so that log zeta is single-valued and the residual argument lies in the nearest-integer window. The two results therefore expose a precise missing regularity step: sample existence does not provide the boundary certificate required for exact zero counting.",
